    "body": "## What does this implement/fix?\n\n| Property | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Method | `BrowserAuthScreen` |\n| Class | `sh.hnet.comfychair.ui.screens.BrowserAuthScreenKt` |\n| File | `app/src/main/java/sh/hnet/comfychair/ui/screens/BrowserAuthScreen.kt` |\n| Specification | `WebViewSslErrorBypassSpec` |\n| Analysis tool | `static:manual` |\n\n## Summary\n\n### Observed dynamic-analysis failure\n\nThe dynamic-analysis run by `static:manual` reported (`WebViewSslErrorBypassSpec`) while exercising `sh.hnet.comfychair.ui.screens.BrowserAuthScreenKt.BrowserAuthScreen`:\n\n> Static candidate (not executed): the WebViewClient of the server login screen overrides onReceivedSslError and calls handler.proceed() unconditionally, so any certificate error -- untrusted CA, hostname mismatch, expired -- is accepted while the user types server credentials (CWE-295).\n\n### Root cause\n\nThe WebViewClient attached to the WebView overrides android.webkit.WebViewClient.onReceivedSslError and directly invokes the callback-supplied android.webkit.SslErrorHandler.proceed() without inspecting the certificate error or applying a certificate-specific acceptance predicate. This violates the reported requirement that recoverable SSL certificate errors be cancelled rather than continued. [CLM-001, CLM-002, CLM-003, CLM-004]\n\n### Correction strategy\n\nReplace the unconditional SSL-error continuation with the documented cancellation response in the reviewed callback, preserving the existing WebViewClient association and navigation behavior on paths without certificate errors.\n\n### Coordinated changes\n\n- `app/src/main/java/sh/hnet/comfychair/ui/screens/BrowserAuthScreen.kt`: Keep the public documentation accurate after certificate errors are no longer accepted by the WebView callback.; Cancel WebView requests with recoverable SSL certificate errors instead of unconditionally accepting the certificate.\n- `app/src/test/java/sh/hnet/comfychair/ui/screens/BrowserAuthScreenSslErrorPolicyTest.kt`: Adds the planned source-level JUnit regression coverage, scoped to the SSL-error callback, ensuring the coordinated production change cancels the handler and never calls proceed.\n\n### Verification included\n\n- Run BrowserAuthScreenSslErrorPolicyTest to verify the onReceivedSslError source-level policy.\n- Run the app module Gradle build and unit-test task, including BrowserAuthScreenSslErrorPolicyTest.\n- Compile the patched module's androidTest sources.\n- If a project-owned runtime harness is available, verify that the SSL-error callback invokes cancel rather than proceed.\n- Run BrowserAuthScreenSslErrorPolicyTest through the app module JVM unit-test task.\n- Run the patched app module Gradle build and unit-test task.\n- Compile the patched app module androidTest sources.\n\n## How was this tested?\n\n- [x] Automated test coverage added in 1 test file(s)\n- [x] Project compiles successfully (`:app:testDebugUnitTest`)\n- [ ] Confirmed by a project maintainer\n\n## Additional context\n\nI’m an undergraduate Computer Engineering student at the University of Brasília (UnB), and this contribution is part of a research project involving static analysis of Android apps.\n\nI’m happy to adjust the implementation to better match the project’s architecture, coding conventions, or maintainers’ recommendations.",
